WEBVTT

00:00.660 --> 00:02.240
Problem number six.

00:02.240 --> 00:05.430
Let f be defined by
f of x is equal to--

00:05.430 --> 00:06.360
and we have two cases.

00:06.360 --> 00:10.790
When x is less than or equal
to 0, f is 1 minus 2 sine of x.

00:10.790 --> 00:15.920
When x is greater than 0,
f is e to the negative 4x.

00:15.920 --> 00:19.570
Show that f is
continuous at x equals 0.

00:19.570 --> 00:21.770
So for something to be
continuous at x equals 0,

00:21.770 --> 00:23.730
let's think about
what has to happen.

00:23.730 --> 00:27.570
So if I have a function
here-- So that is my x-axis.

00:27.570 --> 00:31.710
And let's say this is my y-axis.

00:31.710 --> 00:33.790
And we care about what
happens at x equals 0.

00:33.790 --> 00:35.690
So x equals 0 there.

00:35.690 --> 00:39.220
And let's say that
this is our function.

00:39.220 --> 00:42.009
So maybe our function maybe
looks something like this.

00:42.009 --> 00:42.550
I don't know.

00:42.550 --> 00:44.100
Well, this one in
particular probably

00:44.100 --> 00:45.183
looks something like this.

00:48.770 --> 00:51.100
This one in particular
might look-- who knows.

00:51.100 --> 00:53.075
And then it might look
something like that.

00:53.075 --> 00:54.700
And the particulars
are that important.

00:54.700 --> 00:56.700
We just have to think
about what they're asking.

00:56.700 --> 01:03.780
In order for it to
be continuous here,

01:03.780 --> 01:07.740
so the limit as we
approach 0 from the left

01:07.740 --> 01:13.750
should be equal to the
value of the function at 0.

01:13.750 --> 01:18.170
So the limit of f of x should
be equal to f of 0, which

01:18.170 --> 01:20.210
should be equal to the
limit as we approach

01:20.210 --> 01:23.420
from the right, which
should be equal to the limit

01:23.420 --> 01:25.550
as we approach 0 from the right.

01:25.550 --> 01:27.650
So that should be
equal to the limit

01:27.650 --> 01:31.080
as x approaches 0 from
the right of f of x.

01:31.080 --> 01:35.000
And the reason why this matters
is, if this wasn't the case,

01:35.000 --> 01:37.670
if f of 0 wasn't the
same as the limit,

01:37.670 --> 01:40.024
then we might have a
gap right over there.

01:40.024 --> 01:41.065
So you could have limits.

01:41.065 --> 01:43.410
So you could have a
situation like this,

01:43.410 --> 01:45.500
where you have a gap
right over there.

01:45.500 --> 01:47.480
And then it looks
something like that.

01:47.480 --> 01:50.680
So the two limits from the
left and the right both exist,

01:50.680 --> 01:53.070
and the limit at that
point would exist.

01:53.070 --> 01:56.280
But if the function itself does
not equal that value there,

01:56.280 --> 01:58.610
if it equals something
else, then the function

01:58.610 --> 01:59.880
would not be continuous.

01:59.880 --> 02:01.920
So that's why the
limit has to be

02:01.920 --> 02:04.780
equal to the value of
the function in order

02:04.780 --> 02:07.390
for it to be continuous.

02:07.390 --> 02:09.750
So let's think about
whether all of these things

02:09.750 --> 02:10.459
equal each other.

02:10.459 --> 02:13.041
First of all, let's just think
about the value of the function

02:13.041 --> 02:13.790
there.

02:13.790 --> 02:15.940
So remember, we're doing part a.

02:15.940 --> 02:20.240
F of 0 is equal to-- we're
going to use this first case,

02:20.240 --> 02:23.110
because that's the case for
x is less than or equal to 0.

02:23.110 --> 02:32.480
So f of 0 is going to be
equal to 1 minus 2 sine of 0.

02:32.480 --> 02:36.850
Well, sine of 0 is
0, 2 times 0 is 0.

02:36.850 --> 02:38.310
So this whole thing is 0.

02:38.310 --> 02:41.170
1 minus 0 is 1.

02:41.170 --> 02:42.180
Fair enough.

02:42.180 --> 02:48.050
Now let's think about the
limit as x approaches 0

02:48.050 --> 02:49.480
from the left-hand side.

02:49.480 --> 02:54.840
So as we approach 0 from the
left-hand side of f of x.

02:54.840 --> 02:56.960
So as we approach 0 from
the left-hand hand side,

02:56.960 --> 02:59.980
we're dealing with values
of x that are less than 0.

02:59.980 --> 03:03.330
So once again, we're dealing
with this first case up here.

03:03.330 --> 03:06.700
So this is the limit
as x approaches 0

03:06.700 --> 03:12.540
from the left-hand side
of 1 minus 2 sine of x.

03:12.540 --> 03:15.560
Now, sine of x is continuous.

03:15.560 --> 03:16.690
It's a continuous function.

03:16.690 --> 03:19.760
So this is going to be the same
thing as 1 minus 2 sine of 0,

03:19.760 --> 03:23.670
which we've already figured out,
which is exactly equal to 1.

03:23.670 --> 03:24.611
So this is equal to 1.

03:24.611 --> 03:26.860
So the value of the limit
as we approach from the left

03:26.860 --> 03:28.735
is the same as the
value of the function.

03:28.735 --> 03:30.860
Now let's do it for as we
approach from the right--

03:30.860 --> 03:34.720
as we approach from values
of x greater than 0.

03:34.720 --> 03:38.750
So let's think about the
limit as we approach 0

03:38.750 --> 03:41.540
from the right of f of x.

03:41.540 --> 03:43.040
So here, we're
dealing with values

03:43.040 --> 03:44.650
of x that are larger than 0.

03:44.650 --> 03:47.350
So we're dealing with
this case right over here.

03:47.350 --> 03:50.600
So that's going to be the
limit as x approaches 0

03:50.600 --> 03:54.570
from the right of e
to the negative 4x.

03:54.570 --> 03:57.430
And for the x's that we care
about, or actually in general,

03:57.430 --> 03:59.630
this is a continuous function.

03:59.630 --> 04:01.930
This is a continuous function.

04:01.930 --> 04:03.980
So this is going to
be the same thing as e

04:03.980 --> 04:06.210
to the negative 4
times 0, which is

04:06.210 --> 04:08.500
just e to the 0,
which is just 1.

04:08.500 --> 04:10.880
So this, once again,
is equal to 1.

04:10.880 --> 04:13.580
So the function is equal to
1 at that point, the limit

04:13.580 --> 04:15.530
as we approach from
the left is equal to 1,

04:15.530 --> 04:18.440
and the limit as we approach
from the right is equal to 1.

04:18.440 --> 04:22.540
So the function is
continuous there.
